docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:15:`T_git <= declared_issue_time` is precise as a numeric comparison. It is not proof that the assertion was pre-committed before receipt creation, or even before the receipt's anchor in chain order. The receipt can have `T < declared` by ε, while git can have `T_git = declared`. Moreover Bitcoin nTime is not monotone and establishes no UTC lower bound. Calling this the “stricter” choice than `T_git <= T` is wrong for `T < declared`: those rules are incomparable over A9's allowed domain.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:17:**Distinguishing input:** an honestly signed receipt declares 12:00, anchors at height h with nTime 11:55, and obtains its five successors in time. Its declaration is within the signer's ε check. A subsequently manufactured signed assertion anchors at height j > h with nTime 11:59, during the configured repository-key interval. A DNS snapshot anchors at 12:10 with valid witness windows. Use the same genuine receipt keys, matching channel values, and a window containing declared and T. Every proposed temporal comparison passes, although the git assertion was made after the receipt existed and after its inclusion block. A9's channel-capture adversary can manufacture the authority evidence after capturing the relevant channel credentials, while retaining the uncompromised service's original signatures. This is a counterexample to the proposed pre-commitment explanation, not a forgery of those signatures or a demonstration of key reattribution. Capture of both authority channels is the premise of this version of the construction.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:25:If C4's “pre-committed” is intended to mean an externally checked prior authority commitment, also require `h_git + 5 < h_receipt` on the pinned mainnet history and call that **chain-order pre-commitment**, expressly retaining A2.1's UTC limitation. This additional rule needs owner selection and honest-path boundary vectors; it is not already in the draft. If “pre-committed” promises actual pre-issue chronology, author correction/amendment of that registered claim is necessary unless a binding to previously established authority evidence is introduced. Such a binding can change signed frames and P8 vectors. Merely editing E, which A9 incorporates at its signing baseline, cannot evade this decision.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:27:**Label:** split CORRECT (withdraw chronology overclaim), new owner profile restriction (the comparison), and issuer practice (wait for k=6). Do not label the whole package CLARIFY. No honest shipped V1 counterexample to the wait-first revised issue path was found, but “ships complete” alone does not imply wait-before-sign.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:35:It does **not** imply that contradictory evidence becomes harmless, or that A9's coverage outcome can be changed without identifying the change. A proof with qualifying h and an attestation q beyond the pin horizon, but no headers at q, receives VALID_STRICT under G05 if everything else passes. A9 says “Anchors beyond the newest pin are UNVERIFIABLE”; it also says every **bundled header** must be pinned. The latter is clearly respected; whether the former quantifies over selected anchors or all presented Bitcoin attestations is unresolved. The draft chooses selected anchors. That is a possible reading, not evidence that no registered sentence is implicated.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:41:> Attestation selection is existential over qualifying branches. A syntactically valid Bitcoin attestation without a complete h..h+5 segment is nonqualifying, not a required failed check. It supplies no time or trust fact. All supplied headers, including unused ones, are independently checked against pins. For each adjacent supplied pair at consecutive heights, the prev-hash link must match. Missing heights are not a broken-link failure. The selected anchor, rather than every unverified attestation, is the referent of “anchors beyond the newest pin.”
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:43:Obtain an explicit owner reading of A9's C6 sentence; if its intended meaning was universal coverage, record an amendment/correction, rather than silently overriding it. Add conformance cases for a qualifying branch plus absent-q headers, partial-q headers, unpinned-q headers, a contradicted q branch, and pending-only proof. A genuine upgraded proof containing a complete valid original witness should pass when new branches lack headers. No NARROW-MODEL follows from pruning alone; treating this as a loss of cryptographic protection would overstate what all-branch verification can protect.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:49:Lowest height is deterministic once qualification is fixed. It is not equivalent to lowest timestamp. Let two qualifying attestations have h1 < h2, `nTime(h1) = not-after + 1`, and `nTime(h2) = not-after`. Both depth segments and all other comparisons can pass. G03 gives ISSUANCE_WINDOW; smallest-nTime gives VALID_STRICT. Reverse the boundary relative to `declared - epsilon` and the acceptance direction reverses. Nonmonotone nTime is allowed without a deep reorganization or hash break.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:55:> Earliest means lowest Bitcoin block height among qualifying attestations, not least nTime. Both T and confirmed_at come from that selected segment. Nonmonotone timestamps can make another selection rule return a different verdict on the same proof. This profile fixes the chain-order reading of A9 C5.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:65:Append-only CI cannot prevent this when the verifier accepts any validly signed config and the attacker can bypass CI. Even enforcing append-only at the verifier would not stop adding attacker entries and pins at new heights. Conversely, if config hashes are independently allowlisted in an authenticated release, possession of its detached-signature key alone does **not** suffice. The draft must state which distribution model it means. An unsigned config inside an authenticated, integrity-checked release can be trustworthy; “unsigned implies local edits are accepted” is not generally true.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:73:Choose either the dedicated-key update model or independent release allowlisting, and specify bootstrap, key rotation, version/genesis values and invalid-config no-verdict behavior. Keep “distribution compromise” as an explicit external assumption, and test a captured repository signer trying to authorize a config. Generating a third token is insufficient evidence by itself.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:81:The algorithm allowlist is a legitimate visible NARROW-CLAIM, not a demonstrated NARROW-MODEL. Unsupported algorithms must never become an insecure-but-accepted chain. However, the table “per required RRset” says a supported signature validating at T_snap passes and does not include KSK reliance eligibility. G22 supplies byte equality, not the missing period check. Another implementer will import A9's period rule; a literal implementation of the generalized table can pass a root signed by a trusted retired KSK outside reliance. A captured retired KSK can produce such a signature without breaking its algorithm. A9 governs and forbids this acceptance, so the final text must expressly preserve it.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:91:Define DS outcomes explicitly: a supported matching authenticated DS/DNSKEY path is eligible; if no such path exists and an unsupported algorithm/digest could supply the required path, report UNVERIFIABLE/DNSSEC_ALGORITHM_UNSUPPORTED; if all relevant possibilities are supported and checked and none matches, report INVALID/DNSKEY_DS_MISMATCH. Missing necessary RRsets/signatures are unperformable. Preserve P's root reliance/window/crypto reason precedence. Add mixed type-2/type-4, supported+unsupported RRSIG, missing witness, and outside-reliance controls. Replace the rollover example with an actually unsupported algorithm. Record the allowlist as an owner scope choice, with issue-time fail-before-ship handling. No P8 change is required; an amendment is required only if this overrides A9's root rule or registered honest-case scope rather than selecting a supported issuance path.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:97:A failed check makes the verdict INVALID, but does not settle its stable reason or T. On a bundle with both KEY_MISMATCH and a bad git signature, executing git first and stopping reports a different reason from G31's canonical keys-first order. On a bad-signature receipt with otherwise qualifying receipt OTS, one implementation stops before OTS and reports T=null; G32 requires the verified T. The draft permits both schedules. Dependencies on failure can also hide an independently determinable field.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:103:Enumerate subchecks and ties within stages, including all missing member paths and per-slot checks. Define T=null for contradictory/unusable receipt anchor evidence, rather than merely “some qualifying attestation” when another receipt branch contradicts it. Add reversed-schedule tests with two failures, unknown alg plus independent failure, and a signature failure plus a usable anchor. No amendment or P8 byte change is needed to satisfy the existing stable-field contract.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:163:The close-only inequality correctly preserves eligibility previously granted through as_of **if** starts, keys, D, L and prior entries are immutable and as_of is nondecreasing. The draft explicitly freezes finalized ends but does not fully state these other invariants. Changing a KSK's L from 21 days to one day after its end is closed removes a formerly usable witness without deleting the key or changing the endpoint. The release checker is therefore underspecified. Open KSKs also use [start, as_of] rather than [start, as_of+L]; that is a reasonable finite coverage convention but must be expressed as reliance policy rather than silently inferred.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:167:Appending pins can make an earlier attestation in the same proof qualify under G05 and thus change T or even VALID_STRICT to INVALID. Example: initially only h2 qualifies, inside c1; later coverage of h1 < h2 selects T1 before declared−ε. This does not violate A9's exact-output contract for identical declared inputs, because the config changed. It does refute any stronger claim that append-only updates preserve all verdicts merely by preserving witnesses. State this distinction explicitly; do not replace A9's contract with a monotonic-verdict promise. No new NARROW-MODEL is needed for an accurately limited coverage convention. Retired-KSK manufacture during the already adopted L extension is A9's acknowledged residual; an inadvertently longer horizon is a release failure, not permission to claim the exposure is bounded by the real retirement date.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:184:| G02 | Numeric rule unique; chronology meaning remains wrong. | F01: post-receipt assertion can satisfy purported pre-commitment. | Wait-first future issue path can pass; prior complete-at-ship alone does not guarantee it. | CORRECT plus new profile restriction; author/amendment conditional on C4 chronology meaning. F01. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:191:| G09 | Horizon and release invariants incomplete. | No new attack with immutable intervals; stale-open exposure acknowledged, not solved. | Evidence past a particular config horizon is legitimately unperformable. | Delegated endpoint convention; explicit new policy, not proof of actual retirement. F12. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:238:- Prototype `spike/verify_offline.py:249–276` does mark each insufficient-header attestation unperformable while selecting lowest good height, confirming the G05 instrument departure. `real/ots.py:92–95` selects lowest merkle-correct height but by itself does not enforce depth/pins; the draft's complete qualification rule is stronger than that helper.

